+# Sealed data migrations
+
+Use this contract when a release intentionally changes WordPress data. Keep
+`verifyState` read-only; put mutations in the migration entry point instead.
+
+## Configuration and closure
+
+```json
+"migration": { "source": "build/photo-migration", "hook": "run.php" }
+```
+
+`source` is a directory relative to the config root, prepared by the packaging
+command when applicable. `hook` is a relative PHP path inside that directory.
+All regular files are copied to `bundle/migration/` and covered recursively by
+`SHA256SUMS`; symlinks and unsafe paths are rejected. Both local and on-host
+rehearsals consume this identical closure. Configured migrations require the
+owner-confirmed **data** tier even without a Git diff or base commit.
+
+## Hook protocol
+
+The driver calls `wp eval-file migration/<hook> <mode> <release-id>`:
+
+| Mode | Contract |
+| --- | --- |
+| `capture` | Read-only; print a nonempty baseline snapshot to stdout, no progress text. The driver captures it before the paired backup and includes `MIGRATION-BASELINE` in its checksum manifest. |
+| `apply` | Read that exact snapshot from `php://stdin`. Validate target, manifest and release identity; require the release-owned maintenance guard. Perform only the approved changes; support idempotent retry or fail closed on a partial state requiring recovery. |
+| `verify` | Read the same snapshot from stdin. Read-only expected-result/delta checks. Exit nonzero on any discrepancy. |
+
+Hook output must never expose secrets or private record rows. Prefer identities,
+counts and hashes in the baseline. Hooks must not send mail or reach external
+services during isolated trials.
+
+The baseline is never regenerated by install/resume. Backup checksums cover it;
+changing the configuration, closure or baseline invalidates the evidence.
+
+## Fingerprint contract
+
+When a migration is configured, the **recordFingerprint** hook also receives
+`MIGRATION-BASELINE` on stdin, with plugins/themes skipped as before. It still
+prints exactly one 64-hex digest. Rehearsal and trial now compare that digest to
+the original backup value, in addition to LIVE-CHECK and installed state.
+
+A project that intentionally changes previously fingerprinted rows must define
+and regression-test a bounded expected-delta projection. Preserve the original
+full digest and all unauthorized rows/fields; project only the approved changes
+back to their captured values. Never substitute a newly accepted post-import
+baseline or broaden exclusions to unrelated records. Invalid deltas should
+remain inspectable as a different digest so recovery can still inspect state.
+
+This projection is a project responsibility, not a generic permission to ignore
+attachments, products, options or other data. Explain it in the release record:
+`pre == post` then means the protected invariant, **not zero database writes**.
+
+## Execution and failure boundaries
+
+- Capture precedes the paired backup. Rehearsal restores files/SQL and verifies
+  the original fingerprint using the captured baseline.
+- Trial holds maintenance in the isolated copy, installs code, invokes apply
+  and verify, then checks the original protected fingerprint. Failure destroys
+  the isolated copy and prevents live installation.
+- Live install acquires persistent maintenance and saves/checks the original
+  pre-install fingerprint, installs code and calls apply under the guard.
+- Verify/reopen run migration verify again and compare the protected digest.
+  A failed apply/verify never opens the site. Resume uses the same closure and
+  snapshot with fresh authorization; it cannot adopt a changed digest.
+- **Code-only rollback is unavailable when either release has a migration.**
+  Paired recovery restores both data and files under the existing fresh-owner,
+  current-fingerprint, risk-acknowledgement and RESTORE gates.
+
+The self-test includes real data/local and data/on-host releases, a failing
+trial that leaves the target unchanged, a live migration failure retaining 503
+followed by authorized resume, and paired restoration of the migrated option
+and file. Project-specific migrations still require their own native tests and
+trial against that target's restored backup.
